member.remarks:
Implementation notes We will use this action only for testing this example API, generally we do not assign access keys to users in helloID
Example:
POST /accesskeyassignments { "accessKeyId": 1, "userId": 1 }

Implementation notes We will use this action only for testing this example API, generally we do not create access keys with HelloID
Example:
POST /Accesskeys { "displayName": "Card001", "Type": "Employee, Visitor or temporary" "isActive": "true" }

Implementation notes We prefer OAuth for authentication because it’s secure, easy to use, and works smoothly with modern apps and APIs.
Example request:
POST /oauth/token { "client_id": "example-client-id", "client_secret": "example-client-secret" }

Implementation notes We will use this action when an authorization is granted to a user. Since we do not store the result in HelloID, this action does not require a response
Example:
POST /roleauthorization { "roleId": 1, "userId": 1 }

Implementation notes Adds a new user account to the target system. The response must contain the internal database ID since this is the key we correlate on and will be used for consecutive requests to the target system. Therefore, the `id` field is enlisted in the user schema.
Example:
POST /Users { "employeeId": "1000000", "firstName": "John", "lastName": "Doe", "email: "JDoe@enyoi", "active": "false" }
